{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/1482990-new-immortal-type-the-relic-eaters","id":1482990,"title":"New Immortal Type: The Relic Eaters","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mummy: The Curse"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-06-03T17:22:02-07:00","id":1482990,"title":"New Immortal Type: The Relic Eaters","content":"So I made a thing. Feedback is welcome.<br /> <hr /><br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><b>Relic Eaters</b></span><br /> <i>He stood at the back of the tour group. The others clustered around the urn, ogling its artistic merit, its ancient sensibility, its mysterious origin.<br /> All he could think about was the burning light it gave off, visible only to his eyes. It spoke of memory, power, life. Someone had poured their soul into creating this thing. He thought about how it would feel, having that light inside of him instead.<br /> He\u2019d learned a lot, staying alive this long. He knew that others would be after the urn too. He\u2019d have to act tonight, before anyone else had a chance to claim it.<br /> He also knew how to slay a raven and wear its skin as his own. And that people wouldn\u2019t notice a black bird hiding in the rafters when the museum closed.<br /> Tonight, he\u2019d feast.</i><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>Who They Are</b></span><br /> Immortality does not belong to humanity. But humans are always interested in taking that which they do not own. And one of the most dangerous claims to ownership one can challenge is the Judge\u2019s jurisdiction over all artifacts of Irem. Rather than seek out esoteric, internal paths to immortality, a Relic Eater takes the Sekhem he needs from relics. And while he may not know at first that he is stealing from powers far greater than himself, ignorance before the law is not innocence.<br /> Very few thieves simply mend their ways after their first encounter with the Arisen, however. Assuming they survive.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>How it Works</b></span><br /> All Immortals sustain themselves off of some form of refined Sekhem, Relic Eaters just do it a bit more directly and destructively than most. By consuming the Sekhem released by a relic\u2019s destruction, a Relic Eater can reverse the aging process, prolonging their lifespan in a manner somewhat similar to how an Arisen can delay their return to Duat. Fortunately for Relic Eaters, they have no Judges constantly evaluating them for such blasphemy. Not that this makes the Arisen themselves any more forgiving of the act.<br /> How a Relic Eater comes into their capacity to consume Sekhem varies. Perhaps they were a sorcerer who drew too much power into themselves while trying to fuel vestige creation. Perhaps some unclean fiend stole a part of their soul, leaving them with a hunger to fill that void. More than one Relic Eater has described their heart being ripped out by a swarm of black-eyes birds, only to have it replaced with burning Sekhem from a nearby relic. In any case, few are able to resist the lure of eternal youth when a means of achieving it is placed before them. For many, acquiring Sekhem becomes an addiction as well as a means of preserving immortality. If they weren\u2019t already versed in dubious means of acquiring rare items, Relic Eaters soon become so. <br /> Relic Eaters can never peaceably retire. The youth granted by stolen Sekhem always runs out eventually, and a Relic Eater soon learns they\u2019ll need to periodically acquire and consume new relics if they wish to persist through eternity. Fortunately, their hunger for refined Sekhem makes their relic-hunting abilities second only to the Arisen. Since they have no way of \u201cstoring up\u201d youth, successful Relic Eaters may be tempted to keep any excess relics in reserve, but this will always eventually draw the attention of the Arisen. Still others may resolve to go without, allowing themselves to age normally, telling themselves they\u2019ll go peaceably when their time comes. But as old age looms and the lure of Sekhem beckons, they ask themselves, what harm could a few more years cause?<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>Arisen as Foe</b></span><br /> It almost needs no elaboration. Relic Eaters preserve their inferior immortality by blaspheming Irem\u2019s sacred treasures. Their very existence is an affront to the Judges. As a matter of course, they are assumed to serve the Devourer and her ilk, knowingly or not.<br /> Put more pragmatically, a Relic Eater is in direct competition for the same resource as the Arisen. Given a comparative lack of influence and metaphysical might, this means Relic Eaters prefer to hunt in areas where they are at least somewhat sure no mummies are currently active. While Relic Eaters are harder to track than conventional tomb robbers since kepher does not work on relics that have been completely consumed, this does nothing to abate an aroused mummy\u2019s ire. Despite the tempting hoard of Sekhem that the Arisen tend to accumulate, conventional wisdom is to stay as far away from them as possible. Of course, conventional wisdom would suggest not seeking immortality through the destruction of ancient artifacts either.<br /> Even when not seeking relics to consume, Relic Eaters are still better equipped than normal humans to make use of any kind vessel, and are often just as tempted by the power offered by vestiges. This just makes conflict with the Arisen even more inevitable.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>Arisen as Allies</b></span><br /> Although the two are almost inherently antagonistic, Arisen and Relic Eaters can actually find a strange synergy when working together. When Invested, a Relic Eater can sustain their immortality simply by indirect connection to the Lifeweb of their sovereign\u2018s tomb, obviating their blasphemous need to consume relics. Further, their talents for obtaining relics can be channeled towards righteous ends: returning the property of Irem to its rightful owners.<br /> In return, the Relic Eater gains a measure of security. Service to a mummy is (slightly) more predictable than needing to regularly seek out new relics to consume, especially while the mummy is in henet. Plus it provides insurance against other Arisen, who may carry grudges. On the other hand, if the Relic Eater\u2019s sovereign ever decides to return to Duat with all his relics in tow, or otherwise find reason to deplete his Lifeweb of relics, the Relic Eater can find himself tempted to return to old habits, and old antagonism. <br /> <br /> <b>Favored Attribute: </b>Wits<br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Aspects</span></b><br /> <br /> <b>Curse Eater</b>: Relic Eaters are immune to the curses of relics as though they were Arisen (though lacking a Guild); they can simply choke down the backlash of Sekhem to prevent any ill effects.<br /> <b>Eyes on the Prize</b>: Relic Eaters receive the Relic Sensitivity merit for free. In addition, they recognize vessels on sight, as well as their dot rating.<br /> <b>Sekhem Appetite</b>: When draining Sekhem from a relic, in addition to the usual options Immortals have to spend Sekhem dots, a Relic Eater can expend the Sekhem to turn back the clock on their age. Each dot of Sekhem so spent reverts the Relic Eater\u2019s age by a number of years equal to their Sekhem rating. There is no limit to the number of Sekhem dots that can be spent for this purpose at once, though the Relic Eater cannot regress past the cusp of physical maturity (approximately twenty years old) and any excess years are lost.<br /> Additionally, a Relic Eater can consume vestiges like an Arisen, though Pillars are too unrefined to directly increase their lifespan. Relic Eaters can hold a number of Pillar points drained from vestiges up to their Sekhem rating. Any excess points must be spent within three turns or are lost. These stored Pillars are in addition to any gained through Investment or other sources. The Relic Eater can utilize them as an Arisen can, using his Sekhem rating in place of Pillar rating where necessary.<br /> A Relic Eater who suffers a breaking point in the course of pursuing relics may gain a non-persistent version the Addicted Condition, related to consuming vessels, which resolves when he first fulfills the beat-granting condition. On a dramatic failure, he can instead gain the Persistent Addicted Condition.<br /> <b>Thief on Retainer</b>: Invested Relic Eaters do not age so long as there is at least one Relic connected to the Lifeweb of their sovereign\u2019s tomb. Additionally, when both the sovereign and the Relic Eater participate in a kepher Investigation, all rolls made as part of the Investigation gain an exceptional success on three successes instead of five.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#000080\">The longer I study science the more I am convinced that it is <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\">functionally indistinguishable from what our ancestors would refer to as sorcery</span>. And I would know, being both scientist and sorcerer.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23730-scriptorian","name":"Scriptorian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23730_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"181"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}